i='TCS-4876';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.2, PDF page 4: literal-with-replacement distribution and conjectured algorithmic threshold; Section 1.3 distinguishes the overlap-gap results.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Sharp Thresholds for the Overlap Gap Property: Ising p-Spin Glass and Random k-SAT — full version',authors='Eren C. Kızıldağ',year=2025,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.09913',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.09913v2',locator='Version 2, 7 July 2025, first submitted 18 September 2023. Section 1.2, page 5: explicit algorithmic conjecture. The incidental ln(2)/k in Section 1.3 conflicts with this passage and is not the intended threshold.'),dict(id='fix',title='A Better Algorithm for Random k-SAT',authors='Amin Coja-Oghlan',year=2010,url='https://doi.org/10.1137/09076516X',pdf_url='https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~odonnell/hits09/coja-oghlan-random-k-sat-alg.pdf',locator='SIAM Journal on Computing 39(7), 2823–2864. Publisher abstract and 20 February 2009 author-paper introduction: polynomial-time satisfying-assignment algorithm below the stated asymptotic density.'),dict(id='lowdegree',title='The Algorithmic Phase Transition of Random k-SAT for Low Degree Polynomials',authors='Guy Bresler; Brice Huang',year=2021,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.02129',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.02129v3',locator='FOCS 2021; full version 3, 29 October 2021. Definition 2.1: distribution; Definitions 2.2–2.5 and Theorem 2.6: normalized low-degree model and success restriction; Theorem 2.13: local algorithms; Appendix A discusses improvements to the constant.')]
save(i,dict(formal=r'Does the randomized polynomial-time satisfying-assignment threshold for random \(k\)-SAT satisfy \[\lim_{k\to\infty}\frac{\alpha_{\mathrm{alg}}(k)}{2^{k}\ln(k)/k}=1?\] For each fixed \(k\), \(\alpha_{\mathrm{alg}}(k)\) is the supremum of constant clause densities at which some polynomial-time algorithm finds a satisfying assignment with probability tending to one as the number \(n\) of variables tends to infinity. The distribution and quantifiers are specified below.',definitions=r'''Fix an integer \(k\geq3\) and a real constant density \(\alpha\geq0\). On Boolean variables \(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\), generate \(m=\lfloor\alpha n\rfloor\) ordered clauses. Each clause has \(k\) literal positions, each independently uniform among \(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n},\neg x_{1},\ldots,\neg x_{n}\). The formula is the AND of these OR clauses. Sampling is with replacement: repeated literals, tautological clauses and repeated clauses are allowed.

The algorithm receives the entire explicit formula, with variable indices in binary, together with \(n\). It must output one full assignment in \(\{0,1\}^{n}\) satisfying every clause. It may report failure otherwise. The input is sampled without conditioning on satisfiability and without planting or revealing any assignment.

A density \(\alpha\) is algorithmically attainable at width \(k\) if there is a classical randomized algorithm \(A\), with no advice depending on \(n\), such that its running time is bounded by a polynomial in the explicit input length and \(n\) on every input and random tape, and
\[\Pr_{\Phi,A}[A(\Phi)\text{ outputs a satisfying assignment of }\Phi]\longrightarrow1
\quad\text{as }n\to\infty.\]
The probability includes both the random formula and the algorithm's internal randomness. One fixed algorithm must work for all \(n\) at the chosen \(k,\alpha\). Its code, polynomial degree and constants may depend on those fixed parameters. They are not allowed to depend separately on each input length.

Let \(\alpha_{\mathrm{alg}}(k)\) be the supremum of all such densities. Attainment at the supremum is not required. First take \(n\to\infty\) at fixed \(k,\alpha\); only then consider the asymptotic behavior as \(k\to\infty\). Here \(\ln\) is the natural logarithm. This is a leading-order asymptotic question, not a request for an exact critical density at a particular small \(k\).

The target ranges over all randomized polynomial-time assignment-finding algorithms. It imposes no locality, stability, low-degree representation or restriction to a named heuristic. Deciding satisfiability without producing an assignment, satisfying only most clauses, or proving a barrier for a restricted algorithm family is a different guarantee.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion=r'Prove the displayed asymptotic equality, or refute it. The known algorithm gives the lower bound \(1-o_{k}(1)\) on the ratio, so the missing positive direction is the matching upper bound for all polynomial-time algorithms. A refutation must change the limiting leading-order behavior along arbitrarily large widths; an improvement confined to one fixed small width or a lower-order term does not suffice.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Existence persists to substantially higher densities',text=r'For sufficiently large fixed \(k\), the satisfiability threshold is known and is asymptotic to \(2^{k}\ln 2\). This is about existence of an assignment; it does not provide an efficient way to find one throughout that range.',citation='full'),dict(title='An algorithm reaches the conjectured scale from below',text=r'Coja-Oghlan’s Fix algorithm finds a satisfying assignment with high probability below \((1-\varepsilon_{k})2^{k}\ln(k)/k\), where \(\varepsilon_{k}\to0\). Thus the conjecture asks whether its leading asymptotic scale is optimal among all polynomial-time algorithms.',citation='fix'),dict(title='Restricted lower bounds are evidence, with additional qualifications',text=r'Bresler and Huang prove a low-degree obstruction above a constant multiple of this scale; their main stated constant is about \(4.911\), and their appendix discusses improvements. Theorem 2.6 concerns a specified normalized polynomial representation and requires a quantitatively small failure probability, stronger than an arbitrary \(o(1)\) failure rate. Their local-algorithm theorem rules out much smaller success probabilities within a narrower class. Neither theorem covers all polynomial-time algorithms.',citation='lowdegree'),dict(title='An overlap-gap threshold is not the full algorithmic threshold',text=r'The 2025 source explicitly retains the algorithmic conjecture. Its sharp geometric results for random SAT use growing clause width in the relevant exact-solution regime. They do not establish the fixed-width, all-algorithm upper bound asked here.',citation='full')],why='Pinpoints a possible computational barrier inside a regime where satisfying assignments typically exist. A resolution would clarify whether the geometry of random constraint problems predicts the power of arbitrary efficient algorithms, beyond individual heuristics and restricted computational models.',progress=[dict(date='2010',text=r'Fix reaches density \((1-o_{k}(1))2^{k}\ln(k)/k\) with high-probability assignment finding.',citation='fix'),dict(date='2021-10-29',text='The full low-degree paper provides restricted hardness results and explicitly conjectures a sharper constant.',citation='lowdegree'),dict(date='2025-07-07',text='The revised full source still states the all-polynomial-time threshold as a conjecture while proving sharp overlap-gap results.',citation='full')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the conference source and full July 2025 version, the Fix publisher abstract and author introduction, and the full low-degree definitions and principal theorem statements. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution for arbitrary polynomial-time assignment finding. The supremum definition spells out the source’s asymptotic algorithmic-threshold question. The source’s damaged saved density and its separate incidental logarithm typo are not used as mathematical definitions.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered the with-replacement distribution, exact assignment output, success probability, computational model and order of limits. Replaced the placeholder with the explicit conjecture from the surrounding source passage. Separated satisfiability, geometric thresholds and restricted algorithmic lower bounds.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A central statistical-versus-computational gap in random constraint satisfaction, with rigorous algorithms and restricted barriers but no general matching lower bound. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The input is a random conjunction of fixed-width clauses, with all literal positions sampled independently.','The algorithm must find an assignment satisfying every clause with probability tending to one.','The conjectured leading threshold is two to the clause width times its natural logarithm, divided by the width.','Known algorithms reach this scale, while satisfying assignments exist at substantially higher densities.','Existing overlap-gap and low-degree barriers do not establish optimality against every polynomial-time algorithm.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['full','fix','lowdegree'])),notes=['Specified the precise random formula distribution, explicit input, all-clause output and algorithm quantifiers.','Defined the threshold through a supremum and separated the limits in n and k.','Recovered ln(k), avoiding an inconsistent incidental ln(2) formula elsewhere in the full source.','Checked restricted-model and success-probability qualifications in the low-degree lower bound.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
